  "commit": "8ceaa722280a24fefbc9d5a9967fee2a1503812f",
  "description": "A run with no feature in it. Three defects, all of the same shape: a guarantee this project had written down and never actually tested, so each one had been quietly false for its whole life while the thing that was supposed to catch it reported success. The first was found by reviewing the loop's own guarantees adversarially rather than by any test. CLAUDE.md says /data is the only persistent volume and lists SQLite first; the image set no DB_DATABASE, so the database lived inside the image layer and the documented `docker run -d -v doccum:/data` lost every user, permission, grant, file row and search index on any upgrade -- while objects and minio.env survived on the volume, so the instance came back half-alive, which is worse than empty because it looks like it worked. The entrypoint had been creating /data/doccum.sqlite the whole time, a file nothing opened. The smoke's persistence step could not catch it because it ran `docker restart`, which keeps the writable layer: a step named \"Verify persistence after restart\" had passed for its entire life without exercising the property in its name. Replacing the container instead makes the two halves check each other, and neither alone would have proved anything -- without the DB_DATABASE default the verify phase cannot log in at all, because the account is gone. The second was not discovered so much as finally read. item/extract-text-flake closed saying its cause was unknown; the diagnostic that item itself added printed the cause on main twice within 25 minutes of it closing, as \"General error: 5 database is locked\" on an update to the jobs table. SQLite write contention, not the object-store race everyone assumed: four writers on one file with queue, cache and session all on the database driver, and busy_timeout and journal_mode left null, so SQLite's own default busy timeout of zero applied and the loser of a race failed instantly instead of waiting. decision/0010's pattern for the seventh time. Stating the pragmas was the fix; synchronous was named as FULL and deliberately not lowered, because it does nothing for contention and a document archive must not lose the metadata row for an object already in the store. The mutation harness settled both stated values in CI rather than by argument -- present passes, deleted fails -- and the synchronous assertion was deliberately left out of it, because it names the driver's own default and listing it would be exactly the unmutated evidence decision/0012 forbids. The container then printed the values off the real /data database: journal_mode=wal, busy_timeout=5000ms. The third defect was the loop's own. It had reported \"main is green\" every iteration while never once looking at main: the merge step read the pull request's checks and nothing afterwards looked again. Counted rather than assumed, of main's 38 tests.yml push runs up to run 0012, 8 failed and 6 were cancelled, and all five failures after CI was established were merge commits whose pull requests had been fully green. Adding the step exposed a further hole within minutes of writing it, which is the most useful thing in this run: merging pull requests 94 and 95 close together let GitHub's concurrency group cancel the first merge commit's run in favour of the second, so ba799a9 carries two check runs, both green, and a poller asking \"are this commit's checks all green\" gets yes. A false green produced by correctly following the new rule. Only the newest main run speaks for main, and counting the checks is part of reading the answer rather than an optimisation. Three defects, three things that reported success while proving nothing: a restart that could not disturb what it claimed to test, a diagnostic nobody read, and a green nobody sourced. v1 stays at 19 of 42 -- nothing here moved an item -- and item/files-three-pane is next.",
